      "math_academy_id": "q-129152",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}[4f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-2⟩$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-1,\\,2⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}[4f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t=4{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}[4f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-3{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4⟨-\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-2⟩-3⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-6,\\,-8⟩-⟨2,\\,-3,\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,-3,\\,-14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
